      "side": 1,
      "message": "To clarify, this patch preserves the two existing watch registration sites. Before this change, both registered without an errcb, so an expired watch was neither re-established nor reported through an error callback:\n\n- RADOS_URLS watches the common config object (conf-nfs.\u003ccluster\u003e with mgr/nfs) to trigger online export reloads. Restoring automatic configuration updates after watch expiry is the main purpose of this change. Once a disconnect lasts long enough for the OSD to expire the watch, reconnecting alone does not restore it. Subsequent mgr/nfs notifications can no longer trigger configuration reloads, even after connectivity returns. Ganesha has no periodic configuration reload, so export changes remain unapplied until a manual reload or restart.\n- rados_cluster grace uses its watch for wakeups. The RADOS grace object is authoritative, the reaper polls periodically—normally every 10s—and startup waits for cluster-wide grace enforcement. With grace-DB operations working, missed notifications add latency without bypassing that enforcement barrier.\n\nPS3 clarifies the commit message and removes healthy-watch polling. Error callbacks initiate recovery, with backoff for failed re-registration attempts. After re-registration, the URL reconcile hook requests a configuration reload to pick up changes missed while the watch was down.\n\nTested on vstart (Ceph main), pausing Ganesha for 42s so the OSD expires both watches. Without this change, a subsequent ceph nfs export create is not applied until a manual SIGHUP. With PS3, both errcbs fire after resuming, both watches are re-established, and exports are reloaded.\n\nBroader error handling is still needed for recovery-client blocklisting, RADOS operation timeouts, and notifications missed without a watch error. These issues require separate follow-up work; this patch focuses on re-establishing expired watches and reconciling state afterward",
